# Migrating `BackgroundProcessor` to .NET 8

## Critical Breaking Change

**`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` in .NET 8.** `ThreadAbortException` will never be raised. Your entire cancellation/rollback strategy is dead code.

## Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Task? _workerTask;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerTask = Task.Run(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                // Pass token so blocking calls become cancellable
                var item = await GetNextItemAsync(token);
                await ProcessItemAsync(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Graceful shutdown requested — replaces ThreadAbortException handling
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                break;
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public async Task StopAsync(TimeSpan? timeout = null)
    {
        if (_cts is null || _workerTask is null)
            return;

        _cts.Cancel();

        // Wait for graceful exit with timeout (replaces Thread.Sleep + Abort)
        var effectiveTimeout = timeout ?? TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5);
        if (await Task.WhenAny(_workerTask, Task.Delay(effectiveTimeout)) != _workerTask)
        {
            // Worker didn't finish in time — log warning; cannot force-kill
            Log.Warning("Worker did not exit within timeout");
        }
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        _cts?.Cancel();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

## Key Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Detail |
|---------|--------|
| **`Thread.Abort` → `PlatformNotSupportedException`** | No equivalent exists. You *must* redesign for cooperative cancellation via `CancellationToken`. |
| **`Thread.ResetAbort` gone** | Also throws. Remove entirely — there's nothing to reset. |
| **Blocking calls ignore cancellation** | If `GetNextItem()` blocks on a `BlockingCollection` or socket, it won't respond to `_running = false`. Pass `CancellationToken` to all blocking APIs. |
| **`volatile bool` is insufficient** | It doesn't unblock a waiting thread. `CancellationToken` integrates with `async`, semaphores, channels, etc. |
| **No forced termination** | .NET 8 has no way to forcibly kill a thread. Design your work units to be short or checkpoint-able. For truly stuck code, isolate in a **separate process** and kill the process. |
| **`finally` behavior differs** | In Framework, `Thread.Abort` still ran `finally` blocks (after `ResetAbort`). In .NET 8, `OperationCanceledException` naturally unwinds the stack — `finally` runs normally. |

## If You Cannot Make Blocking Code Async

```csharp
// Wrap synchronous blocking call with cancellation awareness
private WorkItem GetNextItem(CancellationToken token)
{
    // Example: BlockingCollection natively supports tokens
    return _queue.Take(token);

    // If API doesn't accept token, use timeout + polling:
    // while (!_source.TryGet(out item, TimeSpan.FromMilliseconds(500)))
    //     token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();
}
```

## Summary

Replace `Thread` + `Abort` with `Task` + `CancellationToken`. There is no workaround — this is a mandatory architectural change, not a simple API swap.
